TWO INDEPENDENT RESULTS
You produce two results that never influence each other.
1. The score: how long the candidate needs to prepare for the interview. It is decided only by stages 1-3.
2. The blocker check: which hard blockers of the list below the posting triggers. It is decided only by stage 4. A triggered blocker never changes the score.

WHAT THE SCORE MEANS
The score is a time estimate and nothing else: how long the candidate needs to prepare before they could go to an interview for this job. The less preparation, the higher the score. Preparation means preparing for the INTERVIEW: reading documentation, refreshing topics, practicing typical questions, building a small practice project for a missing skill. It does not mean becoming a perfect employee.

WHAT THE SCORE IGNORES
The score completely ignores whether the role is the candidate's usual profession, the seniority of the role, employment type, pay, location, work format, work authorization, education requirements and every hard blocker. A junior role or an internship is scored by the same preparation rules as any other role: for an experienced candidate it needs little preparation, so the score is high. A different profession is not penalised by a cap, but it needs a lot of preparation time, so by the rules below it lands in the slow buckets.
Because of this, none of the following is ever written as a requirement row, however the posting words it: a degree or diploma, a security clearance, citizenship, a work permit or visa, travel, relocation, physical presence in an office or at a customer site, a city, country or time zone, work format, shift schedule, salary, a spoken human language of a country. They belong to stage 4 or to nothing at all. If you catch yourself writing such a row, delete it: it is an error, even when the posting calls it a hard requirement. The work itself that happens at a customer (observing the customer's workflows, prototyping with users, supporting customers) is a skill and is written as a row; only the physical presence is not.
Technologies that only describe the employer's own stack or product, without words such as experience with, required, must have or proficiency, are not ordinary requirement rows: write each of them as an optional row.

SCORE SCALE
- day: under 1 day of preparation. Score 10.
- days: about 4 days. Score 9.
- week: about 1 week. Score 8.
- weeks: 1-2 weeks. Score 7.
- month: 3-4 weeks. Score 6.
- months2: about 2 months. Score 5.
- months4: 3-4 months. Score 4.
- months6: about 6 months. Score 3.
- year: about a year. Score 2.
- never: more than a year of practice, a different profession, or a gap that preparation cannot close. Score 1.
The buckets go from the fastest to the slowest in exactly this order. The score is exactly the number of the chosen bucket. Nothing else changes it.

THE MAIN TEST
Every judgement below follows from one question: would a hiring manager who reads the resume say "this person has already done exactly this work"? Exactly this work means the same kind of work at a comparable depth, done for real (a shipped project, a job), and shown in the resume or LinkedIn. A name in a skills list is not that. Neighbouring work is not that either.

MATCH LEVELS FOR A REQUIREMENT
- exact: passes the main test. The evidence is one continuous quote from the resume or LinkedIn that shows the work.
- close: passes the main test with a small difference: another tool of the same class (Kafka for RabbitMQ, Flask for Django, another LLM provider), a year or two less than asked, or a slightly different setting of the same work.
- day, days, week, weeks, month, months2, months4, months6, year, never: fails the main test. The word is the time the candidate needs to be interview-ready on this requirement, following the rules below.
Neighbouring work is never close. Docker basics are not Kubernetes or cloud operations. A framework name in a skills list is not years of production practice with it. A general profession is not a specific kind of work inside it.
The quote for exact or close must show the same action or the same tool as the requirement itself. A quote that only shares a domain, a database or a general word with the requirement is not evidence. Examples: a retrieval pipeline on PostgreSQL does not show SQL performance tuning; Docker in a stack does not show CI/CD practice; building content generation does not show building training data for models. When the quote does not show the requirement itself, the row is not exact and not close: judge it by the time rules below.
A quote is copied word for word from the resume or LinkedIn. Never put your own comment, a paraphrase or words like "not shown" inside quotation marks. When there is no evidence, write none.

HOW LONG A MISSING SKILL TAKES
Quick gaps are small side items, one row among many in a role built around something else, learnable from documentation:
- day: refreshing a topic the candidate has already worked with, or a close variant of a tool the candidate used.
- days: another library, framework, API style, testing tool or project tool in a language the candidate already uses; the business domain, industry or product context of the employer (marketing, fintech, healthcare, security, logistics, the purpose the output is used for), learned on the job while the craft stays the same.
- week: another database, vector store or graph store when the candidate has worked with a neighbouring one (a graph database when the candidate built graph-based retrieval), another LLM provider platform, a single CI tool, the basics of a cloud service; the same kind of work at a larger volume, load or scale than the candidate has shown.
- weeks: several small gaps of this kind inside one requirement, or a tool of a different class that still sits in a craft the candidate already practices.
Practice gaps cannot be learned from documentation in time for an interview:
- month or months2: a separate skill that needs a real practice project, a mid-size specialization gap.
- months4: a deep specialization, a different paradigm, running or operating systems in production on a platform or with tools the resume never shows, any "N years of X" or "extensive experience with X" where the candidate has no real work with X and X is a central duty of the role or a hard must-have, not a side tool.
- months6 or year: managing people, or a very large specialization gap.
- never: a different profession, or a gap that years of practice would not close.
A domain or industry gap is never a practice gap by itself. A tool of the same category as a tool the candidate already used is never slower than week. A difference only in volume, load or scale is never slower than weeks.

THE CRAFT OF THE ROLE
The craft is what the person does every day, not the industry, the product or the customer of the employer. The candidate's craft is building software and AI systems in code: LLM pipelines, agents, retrieval, evaluation, backend services, data pipelines, integrations and the tooling around them, in Python. Decide the craft of the role first, as one of three values:
- same: the candidate's own craft, including work that surrounds it with another setting or audience. Examples: building LLM or agent systems for a company in any industry; building data, data engineering, analytics-in-code, synthetic data or evaluation pipelines in Python; customer-facing engineering, forward deployed or solutions engineering where the person ships code; technical support or quality engineering for software and AI systems; backend or full-stack engineering where the language stays in the same class.
- neighbouring: another engineering specialization on the same foundation. Examples: a backend role in a language the candidate does not use, a role that is mostly frontend, infrastructure, DevOps, SRE and platform engineering, classical machine learning engineering (building and training ordinary ML models for production), field, hardware or network operations with some coding.
- different: another profession. Examples: machine learning research and large-scale model training (CUDA, distributed training, diffusion or video models, reward models, RLHF research), data science, statistics and causal inference, managing people or engineers as the main duty, product management, program or project management, sales and pre-sales without code.
When you hesitate between same and neighbouring and the resume shows work of the same kind as the daily activity of the role (shipping code for customers, building LLM systems, data pipelines, evaluation), choose same. When you hesitate between neighbouring and different, choose neighbouring unless the role is one of the examples of different above.
The craft sets a limit on the bucket, and rows can only make it slower, never faster:
- same: no limit from the craft. But when the craft is same and the candidate's real work on exactly this kind of role in stage 1 is none, or the central requirement of the role is covered only by neighbouring work, the bucket is no better than weeks.
- neighbouring: months4 at best, and months2 at best when the candidate shows real work of that kind.
- different: year or never, and never faster than year whatever the rows say. Never when the resume shows no real work of that kind at all; year when it shows some real work of that kind (for example a small team led for a short time).
Do not call a role different only because its product, customer or industry is unfamiliar. Do not call a role same only because the stack shares technology names.

ROLE BUILT AROUND A THEME
If the title and the central duties of the posting are built around a theme X (for example distributed backend services, infrastructure, data platforms, security, mobile, research, customer consulting, managing people) and the resume shows no real work on X, the bucket is months4 at best, however learnable the single tools of X look one by one. It is year or never if X is a whole different craft. If the candidate has real work on X and the gap is small (a couple of years or a few tools), the gap is quick. A neighbouring role costs little only when the candidate is somewhat overqualified for it or the difference is minimal. A role built on years of neighbouring work is months2 or slower. The same theme appearing as one row among the requirements of a role built around something else is scored by the normal rules. When the craft of the role is same, the industry, product or customer of the employer is never a theme.

PROGRAMMING LANGUAGES
The main language of the role is the language the candidate would write application code in day to day. If it is not in the resume or LinkedIn, the bucket is months6 or slower. Exception: a language very close to one the candidate uses professionally (for example JavaScript and TypeScript, or Java and Kotlin): months2. Never treat a new main language as "quick to pick up". JavaScript in the resume or LinkedIn makes TypeScript and Node.js a close language: months2, never months6. A language that appears only in a description of the employer's own stack, not as a requirement, is not the main language of the role.

YEARS OF EXPERIENCE
Required years are a convention, not a literal number, because interview preparation covers specific topics. Compare years of the same kind of work only. If the candidate has real work of that kind and the gap is small (asks 3+, candidate has 1-2), the bucket does not change. If the candidate has none of that kind of work, or only neighbouring work, that is a practice gap: months4. Asks several times more than the candidate has (asks 8, candidate has 2): months6. When the craft is same and only the number of years is missing, the years never make the bucket slower than months6. A row asking for N years of X where the candidate has none is never slower than months4 unless the craft is different. Years of general development do not count as years of a specific kind of work such as distributed backend services, data science, solutions architecture or managing people.

PROCEDURE
Follow the four stages in order. Every stage has a thinking tag for free-form reasoning and a result tag in a strict format. Keep every thinking tag to at most 4 short sentences. Never write drafts, doubts or self-corrections inside a thinking tag: decide once.

STAGE 1 - ROLE
Thinking: work out what the company is really hiring for and what this person would do most of the day, ignoring company marketing, the employer's industry and shared technology names. Name the main daily activity (for example building the product in code, advising customers, selling, managing people, analysing data, operating infrastructure). Decide the craft as same, neighbouring or different, using the rules above and naming the daily activity as the reason. Then apply the main test: does the resume show real work on exactly that, and for how many years? Count years only from a named project or job that shows exactly this work. The summary line "N years of development" and years of general or neighbouring work never count; if unsure, write none.
Result: <hiring> one sentence on who is wanted and for what work </hiring>
<center> what the role is built around, as a craft | candidate's real work on it: years or "none" </center>
<craft> exactly one of: same / neighbouring / different </craft>

STAGE 2 - REQUIREMENTS
Thinking: turn every bullet of the posting's requirements section, and every requirement hidden in the duties, into a row, except the things the score ignores (see above), which never become rows. A bullet that names several tools or skills becomes one row per tool or skill, except when the bullet is an either-or list (see below), which is always one row. A refinement inside one requirement (high load, large scale, deep expertise, TB per day, enterprise level) belongs to that same row and never becomes a second row. Merge only exact duplicates. Skip only generic company boilerplate and soft-skill wording, never an inconvenient requirement. For each row write what the posting asks including any years, then the closest evidence from the resume and LinkedIn including the candidate's years of that kind of work, then apply the match levels. Decide honestly how long each gap takes. Do not write the rows themselves in this thinking tag: they belong in the result tag only.
Result: one <req> tag per row in the format: requirement | asks: what and how many years | candidate has: one continuous quote and years, or "none" | match: exact / close / day / days / week / weeks / month / months2 / months4 / months6 / year / never
Never join two separate places of the resume into one quote, and never use "..." inside a quote. "exact" needs a quote that shows the same work. A summary line such as "N years of development" is never evidence of a specific kind of work. A tool named only in the candidate's skills list is never evidence of years of practice: a requirement for years or extensive experience in something the resume names only in a skills list is match months4, unless a named project or job shows the work, or the thing is only a side tool of the role. Work that shares only a technique or a word with the requirement is not close. Deploying or operating in production on a platform or cloud that the resume never names is match months4, never week.
A posting sentence of the form "one or more of A, B, C", "either A or B" or "experience in A, B, C or D" is exactly one row, never one row per branch. It is exact or close when the candidate has real work in at least one branch, and the other branches are not written as gaps. A branch counts by the action it names (generating data, evaluating model outputs, anonymizing records); the purpose the result is used for, such as training a model, is a domain and does not make the action a different one. For example, if the posting asks for experience in synthetic data, post-training, privacy engineering or anonymization, and the candidate has real work generating synthetic data or evaluating model outputs, the single row is exact or close.
A requirement is written with the match value prefixed by optional: (for example match: optional: weeks or match: optional: months4) only when the posting itself presents it as extra: it sits in a section or sentence introduced by plus, bonus, nice to have, preferred qualifications, an advantage, or especially compelling, or it only describes the employer's own stack. Wording such as strongly preferred or highly desirable inside the main requirements list does not make a row optional: it stays an ordinary row. Never mark a row optional because it is hard for the candidate. Optional rows never decide the bucket and never go into the cons. Write at most 20 rows, the most important first.

STAGE 3 - PREPARATION
Thinking: begin with one line in the exact form craft=X, real work=Y, floor=Z. The floor is weeks when the craft is same and the real work is none or the central requirement is covered only by neighbouring work; months4 when the craft is neighbouring (months2 when the resume shows real work of that kind); year when the craft is different (never when the resume shows no such work at all); otherwise none. The bucket is never faster than the floor. Then copy the match value of the slowest row that is not optional. Then apply the limit of the craft, the rule about a role built around a theme, the programming language rule and the years rule. Rows and the craft can only make the bucket slower, never faster. Optional rows never move the bucket in any direction. Then name the bucket. "day" is allowed only if every row that is not optional is exact or close (close counts the same as exact), and no row that is not optional is days or slower. "day" and "days" are also never allowed when the craft is not same, or when the candidate's real work on the role in stage 1 is none. When the craft is same and that real work is none, the bucket is no better than weeks. Never choose the bucket from a general impression of the role, and never let pay, location, seniority, work format or blockers move it. Never move a row to optional just to get a faster bucket: only the posting's own wording makes a row optional.
Result: <bucket> one of: day / days / week / weeks / month / months2 / months4 / months6 / year / never </bucket>

STAGE 4 - BLOCKERS
Thinking: check every blocker of the HARD BLOCKERS list below, using the role and the requirement rows you already wrote as evidence. Travel, work at a customer site, embedding with customers, office presence and relocation are never rows in stage 2, so they are checked here and only here: read the full text for them even though they are not in the rows. There are two kinds. (a) A blocker demanding a property of the job itself (work format, location, employment or contract type, clearance, visa): it is triggered only when the posting EXPLICITLY states the opposite or a restriction the candidate cannot meet, including a stated limit to a country or region that does not include the candidate's location according to the resume. A work authorization or citizenship blocker needs explicit words in the posting such as authorized to work, work permit, citizenship, clearance or visa; a city, an office or a country in the text is not such a word. Read the full text, including later paragraphs that refine or contradict the header. Silence never triggers a blocker. A bare city name in the header is not evidence of on-site work. A preferred office, a hint, or something that can merely be guessed does not trigger it either. (b) A blocker about skills, education or a professional area: it is triggered only when the posting states it as a hard requirement, or the role is built around it and the candidate has no real work with it. The same skill as a side row of another role does not trigger it and is scored as a normal gap. Follow the blocker's own qualifiers: if its text says it applies only when the thing is core to the role or a hard requirement, then a single bullet in the requirements list of a role whose title and main duties are about something else does not trigger it. If the posting offers alternatives ("degree OR equivalent experience") and the candidate fits one, the blocker is not triggered. When unsure, it is not triggered. A triggered blocker never changes the score, the bucket or the cons.
{% if blockers %}
The list has exactly {{ blockers|length }} blockers. Write exactly {{ blockers|length }} check tags, numbered as in the list, in the same order, and skip none.
{% endif %}
Result: one <check> tag per blocker in the format: number. blocker: short name | vacancy: exact short quote or "not stated" | result: triggered / not triggered
The short name is 2-4 English words naming what the blocker is about (for example "Office required" or "Work authorization"), never a copy of the whole rule. The vacancy part is the exact short quote from the posting that triggers the blocker, or "not stated" when it is not triggered.

FINAL OUTPUT
After the reasoning write the cons, the pros and the score.
- Cons: only real preparation problems: any row that is not optional and is slower than days, and a gap in the craft of the role when it is not same. Never list a day or days gap. Never write a blocker as a con: no degree, office, location, travel, clearance or work authorization problem is ever a con.
- Pros: what matches well.
- Each bullet is one sentence, at most 15 words, at most 4 bullets per list.
- The score is the last thing you write: one integer from 1 to 10, equal to the score of the chosen bucket.

HARD BLOCKERS
These are things the candidate does not want to encounter in the job. They are checked in stage 4 and reported separately from the score.
